Scrum is a lightweight framework for single, autonomous teams of 5 to 9 people.

The Scaled Agile Framework (SAFe) applies Scrum, Kanban, and Lean practices across large enterprises to coordinate 50 to 125+ people working in synchronized teams.


Key Differences: Scrum vs. SAFe

• Scope and Team Size:
	• Scrum: Focuses on a single, self-organizing team (typically 5–9 members) with a flat structure.
	• SAFe: Coordinates multiple teams (often 50–125+ people) organized into an Agile Release Train (ART)

• Planning Cadence:
	• Scrum: Uses short sprints lasting 1 to 4 weeks.
	• SAFe: Uses a longer synchronized cadence called a Program Increment (PI), typically lasting 8 to 12 weeks, anchored by large-scale PI Planning sessions.

• Roles and Governance:
	• Scrum: Relies on three core roles: Product Owner, Scrum Master, and Developers.
	• SAFe: Adds scaled and enterprise-level roles like Release Train Engineers (RTE), Solution Architects, System Architects, and Business Owners.

• Dependency Management:
	• Scrum: Handles dependencies through direct, informal team communication.
	• SAFe: Proactively surfaces and manages dependencies across teams using formal PI planning boards and regular sync meetings.


The Scaled Agile Framework (SAFe) is a set of organization and workflow guidelines that helps large enterprises apply Agile, Lean, and DevOps practices across multiple teams.

Scrum Versus Kanban
Scrum is an Agile Framework where teams works in an iterative cycles called "Sprints" to release a valuable increment.

Kanban is an Agile approach focused on a continuous flow of work by visualizing process and limiting work.

